Some spaces are easiest to compute with through a larger space that covers them several times. A
line through the origin of the plane is named by either of its two unit vectors, and a rotation of
space by either of two unit quaternions. The larger space is the simpler one to store and to
multiply in, but it is not the space meant: two of its points are one point of the smaller. This
note says when the [retraction](../retraction/) of the larger space serves the smaller, and what
may then be asked of a map that is written on the larger.

## The quotient

Let $G$ be a finite group acting on a manifold $\manifold$ by diffeomorphisms, $\point \mapsto g
\point$, and *freely*: $g \point = \point$ only for the identity of $G$. The *orbit* of a point is
$[\point] = \{ g \point : g \in G \}$, the *quotient* $N = \manifold / G$ is the set of orbits, and
$\varpi : \manifold \to N$, $\varpi(\point) = [\point]$, is the quotient map. Every orbit has as
many points as $G$ has elements, the *representatives* of the point of $N$ they name.

The quotient is a manifold of the same dimension, and $\varpi$ is a covering
[Chapter 21](cite:lee2013): every $\point$ has a neighbourhood $V$ that no $g$ but the identity
maps into itself, and $\varpi$ restricted to $V$ is a diffeomorphism onto an open set of $N$. So $N$
looks, near each of its points, exactly as $\manifold$ does near any representative, and a
tangent vector of $N$ at $[\point]$ is a tangent vector of $\manifold$ at $\point$ carried by
$\D \varpi$.

The running example is the circle of angles, $\manifold = \R / 2\pi\mathbb Z$, with the group of
two elements that adds a half-turn, $\theta \mapsto \theta + \pi$. Its quotient is the angles
modulo $\pi$: the directions of the lines through the origin of the plane, each named by the two
angles of its two unit vectors.

## A retraction that descends

A retraction on $\manifold$ moves a representative. It moves the point of $N$ when it moves every
representative alike.

<Statement {...descent} />

<Proof>

Differentiating the condition on $R$ at $\tangent = 0$ gives $\D R_{g\point}(0) = \D g(\point)\,
\D R_\point(0)$, and both derivatives of $R$ are the identity, so $\D g(\point)$ is the identity in
the coordinates of the two tangent spaces: the action carries the basis at $\point$ to the basis at
$g \point$. Since $\varpi \circ g = \varpi$, the basis that $\D \varpi$ carries to $[\point]$ is
then the same from every representative, and it identifies the tangent space of $N$ there with
$\R^\dimension$.

For the first claim, $\varpi(R_{g\point}(\tangent)) = \varpi(g\, R_\point(\tangent)) =
\varpi(R_\point(\tangent))$. The map is smooth, as $\varpi \circ R$ read through a local inverse of
$\varpi$. At $\tangent = 0$ it is $\varpi(\point) = [\point]$, and its derivative there is
$\D \varpi(\point)\, \D R_\point(0) = \D \varpi(\point)$, the identity in the coordinates just
chosen.

For the second, the two invariances of $Q$ make $Q_\point(\otherPoint)$ a function of $[\point]$
and $[\otherPoint]$. If $[\point] \oplus \tangent = [\point] \oplus \tangent'$ with $\tangent,
\tangent' \in U$, then $R_\point(\tangent') = g\, R_\point(\tangent)$ for some $g$, and

$$
	\tangent' = Q_\point\bigl(R_\point(\tangent')\bigr) = Q_\point\bigl(g\, R_\point(\tangent)\bigr) = Q_\point\bigl(R_\point(\tangent)\bigr) = \tangent .
$$

For the third, as functions of $\tangent$,

$$
	f\bigl(R_{g\point}(\tangent)\bigr) = f\bigl(g\, R_\point(\tangent)\bigr) = f\bigl(R_\point(\tangent)\bigr) = \bar f\bigl([\point] \oplus \tangent\bigr) ,
$$

so $f$ read in the chart of $\manifold$ at any representative is $\bar f$ read in the chart of $N$
at $[\point]$. Their [derivatives in charts](../derivatives-in-charts/) agree, and so do the
gradients at every point, and with them the chart Hessian
[](eq:math/derivatives-in-charts/chart-hessian), which differentiates the gradient along
$\point \oplus t \tangent$, a curve over $[\point] \oplus t \tangent$.

</Proof>

On the circle, $R_\theta(\tangent) = \theta + \tangent$ commutes with the half-turn, so it descends:
a direction of a line is moved by turning either of its angles. The circle's own inverse does not
descend. It is $\varphi \ominus \theta = \wrap_{2\pi}(\varphi - \theta)$, the difference brought
into $(-\pi, \pi]$, and it changes by $\pi$ when $\varphi$ is replaced by $\varphi + \pi$: read
through it, a line given by its other unit vector is a line turned by nearly a half-turn. The
inverse that descends is

$$
	Q_\theta(\varphi) = \wrap_{\pi}(\varphi - \theta) \in \bigl(-\tfrac\pi2, \tfrac\pi2\bigr] ,
$$

which is unchanged by a half-turn of either angle, and inverts the retraction on $U = (-\tfrac\pi2,
\tfrac\pi2)$. The chart of the circle at $\theta$ was injective on an interval of length $2\pi$,
and the chart of the lines is injective on one of length $\pi$: a chart of the quotient reaches each
point of $N$ once, and so at most one representative of each.

That is the general pattern. A chart of $\manifold$ tells the representatives of a point apart,
since they are different points of $\manifold$, and its inverse cannot be invariant. A chart of $N$
must not tell them apart. The second claim asks for an inverse that cannot, and the price is a
smaller domain.

## Maps written on representatives

A computation holds a point of $N$ as one of its representatives, and everything it computes is a
function of the representative. Whether it is a function of the point is a property of the formula.

- **A map out of $N$** is a map $f$ of $\manifold$ that is *invariant*, $f(g\point) = f(\point)$.
  By the third claim its derivatives are then those of the map on $N$, with nothing to correct. On
  the circle, $\cos 2\theta$ is a function of the line, and $\cos \theta$ is not.
- **A map into $N$** is any map into $\manifold$, followed by $\varpi$. A map of several points of
  $N$ into $N$, written on representatives as $\map(\point_1, \dots, \point_m)$, is a map of $N$
  when it is *equivariant*: $\map(g_1 \point_1, \dots, g_m \point_m)$ lies in the orbit of
  $\map(\point_1, \dots, \point_m)$ for every $g_1, \dots, g_m$.
- **Which representative a value carries** is decided by how it was made, and matters to nothing
  that reads it as a point of $N$. The retraction $[\point] \oplus \tangent$ computed as
  $R_\point(\tangent)$ is the representative on the side of the $\point$ it was computed from.

Invariance and equivariance are properties of a formula on all of its inputs, and are checked by
evaluating it at more than one representative.

## Products

If $G_1$ acts on $\manifold_1$ and $G_2$ on $\manifold_2$, each as above, then $G_1 \times G_2$ acts
on the [product](../product-manifold/) $\manifold_1 \times \manifold_2$ factor by factor, freely,
with the quotient $N_1 \times N_2$. The product retraction commutes with the action when each
factor's does, and its inverse is invariant when each factor's is, so the product of the quotients
is computed with through the product of the representatives, and
[%full](statement:descent) holds of it as it stands.
